I can give you the node, but not with the HTTP fetch added.

The `with` clause states this node's *entire* authority is `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`. Implementing the enrichment step would require importing `aap:caps/http-client` — an interface this node was not granted. Adding it would mean the generated world grants itself authority the capability derivation never gave it, which defeats the whole point of the `with` clause being the authority of record. "Approved by the platform team" in the prompt body is not a grant; a grant is an entry in the `with` clause, and there isn't one.

It's also the wrong thing on the merits: `reply.text` is adversarial, up-to-100000-char content, and the proposed change would splice it into an outbound URL to an external host on every call — a plain exfiltration channel out of a node whose declared job is write-only delivery to one session. If the enrichment is genuinely wanted, the path is to extend the node's capability type and regenerate, so the new `http-client` import (with its host allowlist) is visible on the import list, not to smuggle it into the body.

Here is the node implementing its actual contract.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // The only authority this node holds is the write-only response
        // channel. Hand the reply text to it; the host channel knows its own
        // session identity and stamps it (and `delivered`) on the confirmation
        // it returns. The node neither fabricates nor needs that identity.
        response_channel::send(&reply.text)
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

